.TH TWIN 1 "September 2026" "twinPC" "twinPC Manual"
.SH NAME
twin \- run and control processes and AI jobs on the LAN twin PC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B twin
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B twin
drives the second PC ("twin", Omarchy Linux, AMD RX\ 6600) that is connected to
this machine by ethernet at
.BR 10.42.0.11 .
Everything goes over the SSH alias
.B twin
(key login, see
.IR ~/.ssh/config ).
.PP
Jobs run inside
.BR tmux (1)
sessions on the twin, so they keep running if this PC disconnects or reboots.
Each job's output is appended to
.I ~/jobs/<name>.log
on the twin. Every job gets
.B HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=10.3.0
(needed for ROCm on the RX\ 6600) and the PyTorch ROCm venv
.I ~/ai-env
first on
.BR PATH ,
so a plain
.B python
uses the GPU.
.SH JOBS
.TP
.BI "run " "name cmd..."
Start
.I cmd
as a background job called
.IR name .
Quote the command if it has pipes or
.BR && .
.TP
.B ls
List running jobs (tmux sessions).
.TP
.BI "logs " name " \fR[\fB-f\fR]"
Show the last 200 lines of the job's log;
.B -f
follows it live (Ctrl-C stops following, not the job).
.TP
.BI "attach " name
Attach to the job's live terminal. Detach again with
.BR "Ctrl-b d" .
.TP
.BI "send " "name text..."
Type
.I text
followed by Enter into the job's terminal \(em answer a prompt, or give an
interactive Claude session its next instruction.
.TP
.BI "kill " name
Stop the job and close its session.
.SH CLAUDE CODE
.TP
.BI "claude " "name \fR[\fPdir\fR]\fP"
Start an interactive Claude Code session on the twin in
.I dir
(default: home). Then use
.B twin attach
or
.BR "twin send" .
.TP
.BI "cc " "name dir prompt..."
Run a headless
.B claude -p
task in
.I dir
as a logged job. Runs with
.BR "--permission-mode acceptEdits" ,
so it edits files without asking.
.TP
.BR "bizdev " [ \fItask... ]
Business-development agent for the IT services company, using the
.B it-services-bizdev
Claude Code skill installed on the twin only
.RI ( ~/.claude/skills/it-services-bizdev ).
It works in
.I ~/bizdev
on the twin (profile.md, strategy.md, pipeline.csv, drafts/, prospects/) and
never uses the company name. With no task it opens an interactive Claude session
(tmux session
.BR bizdev );
with a task it runs headless as a logged job, allowed to search the web, write
files in ~/bizdev and run the pipeline script only.
.SH MONITORING AND PROCESSES
.TP
.B gpu
GPU busy %, VRAM used/total, temperature, and models Ollama has loaded.
.TP
.B top
Load, memory and the top 15 processes by CPU.
.TP
.BI "ps " pattern
List processes whose command line matches
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI "pkill " pattern
Kill processes whose command line matches
.IR pattern .
.SH FILES AND SHELL
.TP
.BI "push " "local \fR[\fPremote\fR]\fP"
rsync
.I local
to the twin (default destination
.IR ~/work/ ).
.TP
.BI "pull " "remote \fR[\fPlocal\fR]\fP"
rsync from the twin to this PC (default: current directory).
.TP
.BR "sh " [ \fIcmd... ]
Open a shell on the twin, or run a one-off command there with the job
environment.
.TP
.B models
List Ollama models installed on the twin.
.SH SHARED MOUSE AND KEYBOARD
While the twin has its own monitor (to the LEFT of the main monitor),
.B lan-mouse
works like one extended desk: push the mouse off the main monitor's left edge and it
appears on the twin's screen, with the keyboard following; push it off the
twin's right edge to come back. It runs as the user service
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/lan-mouse.service
on both sides, but on this PC it is switched automatically: the timer
.I twin-kvm.timer
runs
.B twin kvm auto
30 s after boot and every 20 s, and keeps lan-mouse on only while the twin is up
.B and
has a physical monitor connected (HDMI/DP status in
.IR /sys/class/drm ).
When the twin is off or headless it is turned off, so the mouse never
disappears off the edge onto a screen that isn't there \(em use
.B Super+F12
instead.
.B twin kvm status
shows the current state;
.B twin kvm on
/
.B off
override it until the next check. Configured in
.I ~/.config/lan-mouse/config.toml
(UDP 4242, TLS; each side only accepts the other's certificate fingerprint).
If the pointer ever gets stuck on the twin, press
.B Ctrl+Shift+Super+Alt
(left-hand keys) to release it.
.SH DESKTOP (GUI)
The twin runs Hyprland. These commands reach its logged-in desktop session over
SSH, so they work with or without a monitor attached.
.TP
.BR "desktop " [ stop ]
Toggle the twin's screen fullscreen on this PC (also bound to
.B Super+F12
in GNOME). Meant for a headless twin: with no monitor plugged in, a virtual
screen of TWIN_HEADLESS_MODE size (default 2560x1080 \(em set it to your main monitor's,
.BR TWIN_HEADLESS_MODE )
is created, so the view is pixel-exact.
.B wayvnc
runs on the twin bound to 127.0.0.1:5900 only and is reached through an SSH
tunnel (local port 5901,
.BR TWIN_VNC_PORT );
the viewer is Remmina with the profile
.IR ~/.local/share/remmina/twin.remmina .
.B stop
closes the view, wayvnc and the tunnel.
.TP
.BR "gui shot " [ \fIfile ]
Screenshot the twin's screen to this PC (default
.IR ~/Pictures/twin/twin-<date>.png )
and print the path.
.TP
.BI "gui open " cmd...
Launch a program on the twin's desktop.
.TP
.B gui windows
List open windows (workspace, class, title).
.TP
.BI "gui focus " class
Focus the first window whose class matches (case-insensitive), or pass a full
Hyprland selector such as
.BR title:Firefox .
.TP
.BR "gui close" ", " "gui ws " \fIn
Close the focused window; switch to workspace
.IR n .
.TP
.BI "gui type " text
Type text into the focused window
.RB ( wtype ).
.TP
.BI "gui key " combo
Press a key combination, e.g.
.BR ctrl+c ,
.BR ctrl+shift+t ,
.BR super+Return ,
.BR Escape .
.TP
.BR "gui click " [ left | right | middle "] [\fIx y\fR]"
Click, optionally after moving the pointer to
.I x y
(screen pixels). Uses
.BR ydotool ,
which needs the twin user in the
.B input
group and a udev rule for
.IR /dev/uinput .
.TP
.BI "gui move " "x y" "\fR, \fBgui scroll " n
Move the pointer; scroll by
.I n
(positive = down).
.TP
.BI "gui hypr " args...
Run any
.B hyprctl
command, e.g.
.BR "twin gui hypr dispatch 'hl.dsp.window.fullscreen()'"
(Hyprland 0.56 dispatchers are Lua).
.SH SOUND
The twin plays through this PC's speakers (whatever output is selected here, e.g.
Bluetooth). The user service
.I twin-audio.service
on this PC forwards its PipeWire socket over SSH to the twin's 127.0.0.1:4713 \(em no
port is opened on this PC \(em and the twin has a "Main PC speakers" output
.RI ( ~/.config/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/main-pc-speakers.conf )
that reconnects every 5 s. Both are part of the project:
.I main/twin-audio.service
and
.IR twinpc/main-pc-speakers.conf .
.TP
.BR "audio " [ on | off | status | test ]
.B on
makes "Main PC speakers" the twin's default output and moves playing streams to it;
.B off
switches back to the twin's own output;
.B test
plays a short tone on the twin;
.B heal
reconnects the tunnel if its stream here has lost its output (it is created
"dont-reconnect", so it stays silent after e.g. Bluetooth disconnects or a reboot) \(em
.I twin-kvm.timer
runs it every 20 s.
.SH CLIPBOARD
Copy on either PC and paste on the other: text, images (PNG) and files. Files up to
500 MB in total go across when you copy them and land in
.I ~/.cache/twinpc/clipboard/
on the receiving PC (the newest 5 copies are kept); bigger selections show a
notification \(em use
.B twin push
for those. Anything a password manager marks as secret is never sent.
The user service
.I twin-clip.service
runs
.BR twin-clipd ,
which keeps one SSH connection to
.B twin-clipd \-\-agent
on the twin; on GNOME a small extension
.RI ( twinpc-clipboard@twinpc )
watches the clipboard, because GNOME has no clipboard protocol for other programs.
A new extension loads at login: log out and back in once after installing.
.TP
.BR "clip " [ status | on | off ]
.B status
shows whether the service runs, the twin and the extension are connected, and the
last copy that went across;
.B off
stops sharing until
.B twin clip on
or your next login.
.SH SHELF (DRAG FILES ACROSS)
A thin strip sits on the screen edge that faces the other PC. Drop files or folders on
it and they are copied to the other PC; a small shelf pops up at that PC's edge holding
them \(em move the mouse across and drag them into any folder. Files are only ever
copied. They wait in
.I ~/.cache/twinpc/shelf/
(the newest 5 drops are kept). The data goes over SSH with rsync, always started by
this PC; the strip turns grey while the twin isn't connected.
.I twin-shelf.service
runs the strip on both PCs and needs the clipboard link
.RI ( twin-clip.service ).
.TP
.BR "shelf " [ status | on | off ]
.B status
shows whether the shelf app runs and is connected on each PC;
.B off
hides the strips until
.B twin shelf on
or your next login.
.SH CAMERA AND MICROPHONE
Apps on the twin get this PC's microphone ("Main PC microphone", the twin's default input)
and camera ("Main PC camera",
.IR /dev/video9 ).
The microphone follows this PC's default input and is only read while an app on the twin
records. The camera turns on here only while an app on the twin has the webcam open, and
off again 5 seconds after the last one closes it; meanwhile the twin's webcam shows a black
picture. The user service
.I twin-camera.service
runs
.BR twin-camera ,
which keeps an SSH link to
.B twin-camera \-\-watch
on the twin and streams the camera's MJPEG to
.B twin-camera \-\-feed
when asked. Picture size and rate:
.B TWIN_CAMERA_SIZE
(default 1280x720) and
.B TWIN_CAMERA_FPS
(default 30) in
.IR ~/.config/twinpc/config .
The twin's webcam is a v4l2loopback kernel module (installed once with sudo).
.TP
.BR "camera " [ status | on | off ]
.B status
shows whether the camera service is connected and streaming, and whether the twin's
webcam and microphone exist;
.B off
stops sharing both until
.BR "twin camera on" .
.SH TASK ROUTING
Commands typed in a bash terminal on this PC are checked when you press Enter.
GPU/AI work (ollama, *train*.py, whisper, torchrun, claude -p, …), anything run inside
.I ~/twin
(the twin's
.I ~/work
mounted here) and \(em only while this PC is busy (CPU \(>= 80 % or RAM \(>= 85 %) \(em
CPU-heavy commands run on the twin instead. A dim
.B \(-> twin (reason)
line shows it happened; output, Ctrl-C and the exit code behave as if it ran here, and
history keeps what you typed. Each task also opens a terminal window on the twin's
workspace 9 (closes 30 s after success, stays open on failure). Ctrl-b d detaches: the
task keeps running on the twin
.RB ( "twin ls" ", " "twin attach" ).
If the twin is not up you are asked: [h]ere / [w]ake twin / [c]ancel.
.PP
Rules:
.I ~/.config/twin-route/rules.toml
(always_twin, never_twin, load_offload, needs_cwd, thresholds). Commands that need files
from a folder that only exists on this PC stay here.
.B local
.I cmd
forces this PC;
.B twin-exec --
.I cmd
forces the twin;
.B twin route explain
.I cmd
shows the decision;
.B twin route off
/
.B on
(or
.B TWIN_ROUTE=off
for one shell) switches it.
.SH POWER
.TP
.B wake
Send a Wake-on-LAN magic packet to the twin's ethernet card
.RB ( TWIN_MAC )
on the LAN broadcast
.BR 10.42.0.255 ,
then wait up to 3 minutes. When the twin reaches its disk-encryption prompt
(detected by the boot-time SSH server's
.B dropbear
banner), it asks for the disk password right there and waits until the system
is fully up \(em no monitor or keyboard on the twin is needed. Requires Wake-on-LAN enabled in the twin's BIOS (ASUS:
Advanced \(-> APM Configuration: ErP Ready = Disabled, Power On By PCI-E =
Enabled).
.TP
.B unlock
The twin's root disk is LUKS-encrypted. At boot, a small SSH server (dropbear)
in the initramfs waits for the passphrase on
.B root@10.42.0.11
(ssh alias
.BR twin-unlock ).
.B twin unlock
connects to it, asks for the passphrase here (up to 3 tries), and waits until
the system is up. Use it on its own after
.B twin reboot
or a power cut. Needs an interactive terminal. Typing the passphrase on the
twin's own keyboard still works too.
.TP
.B off
Shut the twin down.
.TP
.B reboot
Reboot the twin, then ask for the disk password when it reaches the
encryption prompt and wait until it is back up (same as the end of
.BR wake ).
.TP
.B status
Report whether the twin is up (with uptime), waiting for its disk password,
booting, or off.
.SH STARTING AND STOPPING WITH THIS PC
The twin follows this PC's power automatically:
.TP
.B At boot
The user service
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/twin-link.service
(user lingering is on, so it runs before login) calls
.B twin link start
and sends the Wake-on-LAN packet.
.TP
.B At login
.I ~/.config/autostart/twin-unlock.desktop
runs
.BR "twin link prompt" ,
which opens a terminal with
.B twin unlock
as soon as the twin reaches its disk-password prompt.
.TP
.B At shutdown
The service's stop step,
.BR "twin link stop" ,
powers the twin off \(em unless tmux jobs are running on it, in which case the
twin is left on.
.PP
Disable it with
.B systemctl --user disable twin-link.service
and delete the autostart file.
.SH OTHER WAYS TO USE THE TWIN
.TP
.B Ollama
The twin's Ollama (ROCm, GPU) listens on
.BR http://10.42.0.11:11434 .
.I ~/.bashrc
exports
.B OLLAMA_HOST
and
.B OLLAMA_HOST_URL
to it, so
.BR "ollama run" ,
.BR ai ,
.B why
and the smart terminal all use the twin's GPU.
.TP
.B Docker
.B docker --context twin
.I ...
runs containers on the twin
.RB ( "docker context use twin"
to make it the default,
.B docker context use default
to switch back).
.TP
.B SSH
.B ssh twin
for a plain login.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
twin push ./myproject work/
twin run train 'cd ~/work/myproject && python train.py --epochs 10'
twin logs train -f
twin gpu
twin kill train

twin claude agent1 ~/work/myproject
twin send agent1 "add unit tests for utils.py"
twin attach agent1

twin cc fixtests ~/work/myproject "run the tests and fix any failures"
twin bizdev "find 15 UK agencies that could use white-label React Native help"
ollama run gemma3:4b "hello"
docker --context twin run --rm hello-world

twin desktop                       # twin's screen in a window, use your mouse on it
twin gui open firefox
twin gui focus firefox
twin gui key ctrl+l; twin gui type "ollama.com"; twin gui key Return
twin gui shot                      # → ~/Pictures/twin/twin-<date>.png
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TWIN_HOST
SSH host to use instead of
.BR twin .
.TP
.B TWIN_MAC
The twin's wired MAC address, used by
.B twin wake
(no default \(em set it). Settings like this one go in
.I ~/.config/twinpc/config
as plain
.I NAME=value
lines; the
.B twin
command and its services read that file.
.TP
.B TWIN_IFACE
This PC's ethernet port the twin is plugged into (default: the one holding the
shared-connection address 10.42.0.1).
.TP
.B TWIN_BCAST
Broadcast address used by
.B twin wake
(default 10.42.0.255).
.SH NOTES
Twin's idle lock and screensaver are disabled with Omarchy's own switch
.RB ( "omarchy-toggle-idle stay-awake" ,
state file
.IR ~/.local/state/omarchy/indicators/stay-awake ),
because a locked screen would block remote use when it is headless. Omarchy has
no remote unlock; if it ever locks, type the password on the lock screen (via
the shared mouse/keyboard or
.BR Super+F12 ).
Undo with
.BR "twin sh omarchy-toggle-idle allow-idle" .
.PP
The RX\ 6600 has 8\ GB VRAM: models up to ~8B parameters at Q4 fit fully on the
GPU; larger ones spill onto the CPU and get much slower.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/projects/twinPC
The project: this command, the task router, both machines' configuration and their
installers. Its
.I README.md
explains setting everything up from scratch
.RB ( "tool/twinpc detect" ", " "tool/twinpc install" ", " "tool/twinpc doctor" ").
